<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 10px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Preserving Our Heritage – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Preserving Our Heritage</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> What Should We Preserve in the Future?</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Narrate a story about personal experiences. Interactive: Engage in storytelling sessions using personal experiences.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can narrate a story about personal experiences with cultural traditions, illustrating their importance for heritage preservation (e.g., "During Corpus Christi celebrations in La Villa de Los Santos, I watched traditional dances that showcased our folkloric heritage.")
Can engage in storytelling to describe the personal importance of heritage (e.g., "I visited the Panama Canal, which fills me with pride because my grandfather helped with its construction." "I visited the Panama Canal, where my grandfather worked during its construction.")
Can explain the cultural significance of Indigenous heritage by describing traditional practices (e.g., "The Guna people's traditional use of molas, brightly colored hand-woven textiles, is an important expression of their cultural identity and history.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images featuring diverse Panamanian cultural heritage (e.g., pollera, molas, traditional dances, Panama Canal, Casco Viejo, Indigenous villages). Students are asked to work in pairs and discuss for 2 minutes: "Which of these elements represent our national <b>identity</b>? Have you ever had a personal experience at a <b>site where</b> these traditions are celebrated, or seen an artifact <b>which</b> tells a story about our <b>history</b>?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models narrating a brief personal experience related to heritage using target grammar and vocabulary. For example: "Last year, I visited the Emberá community, <b>where</b> I learned so much about their traditional <b>weaving</b>. It was an amazing experience <b>which</b> made me realize the importance of their <b>efforts</b> in preserving our national <b>identity</b>." The teacher explicitly highlights the use of relative clauses ("where", "which"), past simple ("visited," "learned," "made"), and target vocabulary (e.g., "weaving," "efforts," "identity"). The teacher also models the pronunciation of /eɪ/ in "great," "became," "say" and the reduced vowel in "important," "heritage."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'relative clause,' what kind of words am I referring to? (e.g., 'which,' 'where') What do they connect?" (Expected answer: "They connect information about a noun or place.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'The Panama Canal opened in 1914,' am I using the past simple or present perfect?" (Expected answer: "Past simple.") "And if I say, 'It has attracted many tourists since then,' what tense is that?" (Expected answer: "Present perfect.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio recording (1.5-2 minutes) of two Panamanian teenagers sharing personal stories about their connection to specific cultural traditions or historical <b>sites</b>. The dialogue includes examples of relative clauses, past simple/present perfect, and future simple, along with target vocabulary like "<b>heritage</b>," "<b>culture</b>," "<b>landmark</b>," "<b>preserve</b>," and "<b>important</b>." Register is informal but respectful.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing and interpreting ideas and emotions related to cultural heritage, specifically narrating personal experiences and explaining their significance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (oral):</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What kind of personal experiences are the speakers sharing? What is the main idea of their conversation?" (Students answer orally in pairs, then share with the class.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher provides 3-4 statements on the board about the audio. Students listen a second time and identify which speaker made which statement, or if the statement is true/false based on the audio. (e.g., "Speaker A visited a historical <b>site which</b> was recently <b>restored</b>." "Speaker B believes we <b>will continue</b> to preserve indigenous <b>culture</b>.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Narrating personal experiences using relative clauses ("which," "where"), past simple vs. present perfect, and future simple for predictions. Using vocabulary: "heritage," "history," "sites," "culture," "restoration," "villages," "weaving," "landmark," "legacy," "identity," "efforts," "preserve," "attract," "continue," "restore," "traditional," "cultural," "significant," "colonial," "pre-hispanic." Pronunciation of /eɪ/ and reduced vowels.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion (Oral):</b> The teacher provides sentence stems on the board, and students complete them orally in pairs, focusing on using relative clauses and contrasting past simple/present perfect. (e.g., "My family has a traditional recipe, <b>which</b> __________." "I once visited a historical <b>site where</b> __________." "My grandparents told me stories about a time <b>when</b> __________."). Teacher monitors pronunciation, especially /eɪ/ in words like "say," "great," and reduced vowels in "heritage," "important."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Picture Prompt Storytelling (Oral):</b> The teacher displays a series of diverse images depicting Panamanian heritage (e.g., a "mola" being woven, a traditional dance, a view of the Canal, an ancient ruin). Students, in small groups, choose two images and collaboratively create a short 3-4 sentence narrative about a personal connection or an imagined story related to the images. They must use at least one relative clause and either past simple/present perfect or future simple. (e.g., "This image shows traditional <b>weaving</b>, <b>which</b> is part of Guna <b>culture</b>. I remember my aunt teaching me, and she <b>has made</b> beautiful molas for years.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - "Why it's important" Discussion:</b> Students interview each other using the pragmatic function of "Asking about cultural significance." One student asks, "Why is preserving historic <b>landmarks</b> like [Casco Viejo / a specific traditional dance / Indigenous craft] <b>important</b>?" The other student responds, incorporating target vocabulary and explaining its personal or cultural <b>significance</b>. They should aim to use relative clauses where appropriate. (e.g., "Preserving Casco Viejo is <b>critical</b> because it's a <b>colonial site where</b> much of our early <b>history</b> <b>unfolded</b>. It <b>attracts</b> many <b>tourists</b> and teaches us about our <b>legacy</b>.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Storytelling Circle / "My Heritage Story" Presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students will form small groups (4-5 students). Each student will take turns narrating a personal story (2-3 minutes) about a significant cultural tradition, historical <b>site</b>, or family <b>legacy</b> in Panama. The stories should illustrate the personal importance of heritage and include:
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Description of the experience using past simple and/or present perfect.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of relative clauses ("which," "where") to add detail and context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">An explanation of its cultural <b>significance</b>, incorporating target vocabulary (e.g., "This experience made me realize the importance of their <b>traditions</b> in preserving our national <b>identity</b>.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A statement about how they or others <b>will continue</b> to <b>preserve</b> this heritage in the future (future simple).</li>
                </ul>
                Students are encouraged to use descriptive adjectives (e.g., "<b>traditional</b>," "<b>relevant</b>," "<b>colonial</b>") and express their feelings, demonstrating sociolinguistic competence by "Discussing heritage respectfully" and "Showing interest in cultural diversity" when listening to peers.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Peer Assessment Checklist and Teacher Observation Checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the storytelling circle, students use a simple checklist to provide feedback to their peers on their narratives. The teacher also uses a similar checklist to observe student performance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer & Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation, including target sounds like /eɪ/ and appropriate vowel reduction.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Incorporated at least 5 words from the vocabulary list (e.g., <b>heritage</b>, <b>culture</b>, <b>preserve</b>, <b>identity</b>, <b>significant</b>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar accuracy:</b> Correct use of relative clauses ("which," "where") and appropriate verb tenses (past simple, present perfect, future simple).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Story was easy to follow and presented a clear message about heritage.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration/Interaction:</b> Listened actively to peers and provided constructive feedback.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Oral or written):</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new vocabulary word or grammar structure you used confidently today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you learned from a classmate's story about Panamanian heritage that you found <b>interesting</b>?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (oral, whole class):</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has sharing your personal experiences about heritage changed or reinforced your understanding of its <b>importance</b>?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new insights did you gain today about our cultural <b>legacy</b> from your classmates' stories?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this speaking-focused lesson? Were students actively engaged in narrating and interacting? Did they use relative clauses and varied tenses naturally? Were the target vocabulary words used appropriately? What adjustments are needed for future speaking tasks or scaffolds for students who struggled with fluency or specific grammar points? (e.g., providing sentence starters for less confident speakers; additional pronunciation practice for specific sounds).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4, we will transition from oral storytelling to written composition. Students will use the personal narratives and insights shared today to write an essay describing a significant aspect of Panamanian heritage and its future preservation, focusing on structuring their ideas and applying the grammar and vocabulary practiced in this lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>